Preoperative blood tests were normal except for a slightly elevated white cell count of 11.1 × 1000/µL with neutrophilia. Transthoracic echocardiogram revealed left ventricular hypertrophy with moderate "dilatation": LV end-diastolic diameter (LVEDD) 3.9 cm, left ventricular posterior wall thickness (LVPWd) 0.91 cm, interventricular septal thickness at diastole (IVSD) 1.4 cm. Left ventricular systolic function was preserved with LVEF of 0.55 (Simpson’s biplane method). Aortic root dimensions were normal. Right ventricle was normal in size and function by visual estimation. Left atrium was significantly dilated; right atrial size was normal. Aortic annulus measured 26 mm. The tricuspid aortic valve showed severe stenosis with a median pressure gradient of 29 mmHg and moderate regurgitation with a pressure half-time of 120 ms. Mitral valve showed severe calcification including annulus and leaflets with restricted leaflet motion. Medio-lateral annulus size was 34 mm. Severe mitral stenosis with moderate regurgitation and centrally directed jet was present. Median pressure gradient of mitral valve was 7.1 mmHg. Mitral valve area by planimetry was 1.6 cm2. Mild tricuspid regurgitation was noted. CT scan revealed severe mitral valve calcification including severe circular calcification of the native mitral valve annulus extending into neighbouring myocardium of left ventricle and left atrium. Intraoperative findings confirmed severe calcification of anterior mitral leaflet, both intrafibrous trigones, and extension of calcification into myocardium of posterior mitral valve, left atrium, and left ventricle. Calcifications of native mitral valve annulus and neighbouring myocardium were left untouched except limited removal in posterior leaflet region. In-situ measurement revealed significantly smaller aortic annulus than preoperative measurement. Cardiopulmonary bypass "times": clamping time 142 min, perfusion time 167 min.'
